\label{switching}
Let $D\equiv 1\mod 8$ and $P_D=[w,h,e,\varepsilon]$ be an algebraic cusp prototype. Then $P_D$ and $P_D^\sigma$ are on different components of $\W$. 

In particular, the cusps associated to $\bigl[\frac{D-1}{8},1,-1,-1\bigr]$ and $\bigl[\frac{D-1}{8},1,1,-1\bigr]$ lie on $\W^1$ and $\W^2$, respectively, and are conjugate.
